[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"DataStore (Preferences and Proto) vs SharedPreferences in Android Kotlin"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"For years, SharedPreferences was the standard choice for storing small key-value data in Android applications. However, with the introduction of Jetpack DataStore, modern Android development has shifted toward a safer, reactive, and coroutine-based approach. This tutorial explores the differences between SharedPreferences, Preferences DataStore, and Proto DataStore, providing implementation examples in Kotlin."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding the Alternatives"},{"order":4,"type":"bullet_point","text":"SharedPreferences: Uses synchronous disk I/O, lacks safety against data corruption, throws exceptions on the main thread, and has no mechanism to observe data changes reactively."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Preferences DataStore: Uses Flow to asynchronously store data, ensuring thread safety and preventing UI freezes by executing operations on Dispatchers.IO."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Proto DataStore: Stores data as typed objects using Protocol Buffers. It provides strong typing and schema enforcement while maintaining the asynchronous benefits of DataStore."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"Dependencies Setup"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"To use Preferences DataStore in your Kotlin project, add the following dependency to your module's build.gradle.kts file:"},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"implementation("androidx.datastore:datastore-preferences:1.1.1")"},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"Implementing Preferences DataStore"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"Preferences DataStore uses key-value pairs similar to SharedPreferences, but leverages Kotlin Coroutines and Flow. Below is an example of how to initialize and interact with Preferences DataStore."},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"import android.content.Context
import androidx.datastore.preferences.core.edit
import androidx.datastore.preferences.core.stringPreferencesKey
import androidx.datastore.preferences.preferencesDataStore
import kotlinx.coroutines.flow.Flow
import kotlinx.coroutines.flow.map

val Context.dataStore by preferencesDataStore(name = "settings")

class UserPreferences(private val context: Context) {
    companion object {
        val USER_NAME_KEY = stringPreferencesKey("user_name")
    }

    val userNameFlow: Flow<String> = context.dataStore.data
        .map { preferences ->
            preferences[USER_NAME_KEY] ?: "Default"
        }

    suspend fun saveUserName(name: String) {
        context.dataStore.edit { preferences ->
            preferences[USER_NAME_KEY] = name
        }
    }
}"},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Implementing Proto DataStore"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"Proto DataStore requires defining a schema using Protocol Buffers. First, create a .proto file in your app/src/main/proto directory:"},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"syntax = "proto3";

package com.example.application;

option java_package = "com.example.application";
option java_multiple_files = true;

message UserSettings {
    string user_name = 1;
    int32 font_size = 2;
}"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"Next, implement a Serializer for your proto object and initialize the Proto DataStore:"},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"import androidx.datastore.core.CorruptionException
import androidx.datastore.core.Serializer
import com.example.application.UserSettings
import java.io.InputStream
import java.io.OutputStream

object UserSettingsSerializer : Serializer<UserSettings> {
    override val defaultValue: UserSettings = UserSettings.getDefaultInstance()

    override suspend fun readFrom(input: InputStream): UserSettings {
        try {
            return UserSettings.parseFrom(input)
        } catch (exception: Exception) {
            throw CorruptionException("Cannot read proto.", exception)
        }
    }

    override suspend fun writeTo(t: UserSettings, output: OutputStream) {
        t.writeTo(output)
    }
}"},{"order":18,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":19,"type":"paragraph","text":"While SharedPreferences is deprecated conceptually due to its blocking nature and lack of safety, migrating to Preferences DataStore provides a seamless transition for key-value requirements. For robust applications requiring strict types and complex data structures, Proto DataStore is the recommended modern solution."}]
